### Summary of Key Insights
The recorded evidence shows that Dr. Anthony Fauci did co-author a 2008 scientific paper concluding that the majority of deaths during the 1918–1919 Spanish Influenza pandemic resulted from secondary bacterial pneumonia. However, contrary to the claim, this paper does not mention face masks or mask-wearing at all. Instead, the study identified the primary cause of the bacterial infections as the influenza virus itself, which damaged the mucosal barrier of the respiratory tract and allowed naturally occurring upper respiratory-tract bacteria to invade the lungs. 

### Conclusion
Based on the fact-check, the claim that Dr. Fauci's 2008 paper linked 1918-1919 pandemic deaths to bacterial pneumonia caused by wearing masks is false. While the paper did identify bacterial pneumonia as the primary cause of death, it never mentions masks and instead attributes the bacterial infections to viral damage of the respiratory tract. Therefore, the decision is `refuted`.

### Verdict: REFUTED

### Justification
While Dr. Anthony Fauci did co-author a [2008 scientific paper](https://academic.oup.com/jid/article/198/7/962/2192118) concluding that the majority of deaths during the 1918–1919 Spanish Influenza pandemic were caused by secondary bacterial pneumonia, the study contains absolutely no mention of face masks or mask-wearing. According to reports on the study by [New Scientist](https://www.newscientist.com/article/dn14458-bacteria-were-the-real-killers-in-1918-flu-pandemic/) and [CIDRAP](https://www.cidrap.umn.edu/influenza-vaccines/study-bacterial-pneumonia-was-main-killer-1918-flu-pandemic), the researchers identified the primary cause of these bacterial infections as the influenza virus itself, which damaged the cells lining the respiratory tract and allowed common upper respiratory-tract bacteria to invade the lungs. Consequently, the claim that the paper linked these deaths to mask-wearing is completely false, and the verdict is refuted.